  "recordType": "rule-section",
  "ruleText": "Env-Dw 406.31 Waivers.\n\n(a) Any applicant or supplier of water who would be adversely impacted by the strict application of a rule in this part and who wishes to request a waiver of the rule shall do so in accordance with Env-Dw 202.\n\n(b) Waivers shall not be granted for criteria which pose a direct risk to public health, including but not limited to, proper well construction, multiple wells where required, water quality standards, or the integrity of water storage tank(s).\n\n(c) A waiver requests for a non-community water system’s SPA shall be submitted by the owner as follows:\n\n(1) All requests for waivers shall be submitted as specified in Env-Dw 202 and shall be signed by the owner and the water system’s designer; and\n\n(2) The request shall include information on technically-important factors for a waiver of the SPA such as historical water quality, if applicable, overburdened soil depth, soil type, height and consistency of water table, direction of slope, and potential contamination sources.\n\nSource. (See Revision Note at part heading for Env-Dw 406) #10613, eff 6-1-14; ss by #14158, eff 12-25-24 (formerly Env-Dw 406.28)\n\nAppendix A: State Statutes and Federal /Regulations Implemented\n\nAppendix B: Incorporation By Reference Information\n\nRule Section(s) | Title | Dated | Obtain at:\n\nEnv-Dw 402.06; Table 402-1 | American Water Works Association Standards A100 through F120 | Various | The NH Department of Environmental Services office Or https://store.awwa.org/standards?custitem_facet_standards_field=Current-Standards\n\nEnv-Dw 403.01 | “Drinking Water Treatment Chemicals – Health Effects”, (60) ANSI document identification number NSF/ANSI 60-2024, American National Standards Institute, 2024. | 2024 | American National Standards Institute https://webstore.ansi.org/standards/nsf/nsfansi602021600\n\nEnv-Dw 403.01 | “Drinking Water System Components – Health Effects”, (61) ANSI document Identification number NSF/ANSI 61-2023, American National Standards Institute, 2023. | 2023 | American National Standards Institute https://webstore.ansi.org/standards/nsf/nsfansi612021600\n\nEnv-Dw 404.04(a) | “Recommended Standards for Water Works, Policies for the Review and Approval of Plans and Specifications for Public Water Supplies”, A Report of the Water Supply Committee of the Great Lakes – Upper Mississippi River Board of State and Provincial Public Health and Environmental Managers, 2022. | 2022 | NH Department of Environmental Services office or https://www.health.state.mn.us/c ommunities/environment/water/t enstates/standards.html\n\nEnv-Dw 404.05(p); Env-Dw 405.06(c); Env-Dw 406.06(c) | “New Hampshire Coastal Flood Risk Summary – Part I: Science and Part II: Guidance for Using Scientific Projections\" prepared for the New Hampshire Coastal Flood Risk Science and Technical Advisory Panel, 2019. | 2019 | University of New Hampshire https://scholars.unh.edu/ersc/210 and https://scholars.unh.edu/ersc/211\n\nEnv-Dw 405.17(f), Env-Dw 405.20(j), and 406.19(j) | National Electrical Manufacturers Association Standard ANSI/NEMA MG 1-2021 “Motors and Generators”, 2022. | June 29, 2022 | National Electrical Manufacturers Association (NEMA) https://www.nema.org/standards/view/motors-and-generators\n\nEnv-Dw 405.21(g) and Env-Dw 406.20(h) | American Society of Mechanical Engineers (ASME) “Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code Section VIII-Rules for Construction of Pressure Vessels”, Division 1 BPVC.VIII.1-2023, 2023. | 2023 | American Society of Mechanical Engineers (ASME) https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/bpvc-viii-1-bpvc-section-viii-rules-construction-pressure-vessels-division-1/2023/print-book",
